pl1 = {name:"Basic", attributes:["1 vCPU","1 GB RAM","25 GB SSD","1 TB transfer","Best for: hobby sites, small blogs"]}
pl2 = {name:"Pro", attributes:["2 vCPU","4 GB RAM","80 GB SSD","4 TB transfer","Best for: production apps, small teams"]}
pl3 = {name:"Team", attributes:["4 vCPU","8 GB RAM","160 GB SSD","8 TB transfer","Best for: scaling traffic, multiple services"]}
:::

For most people, **Pro** is the sweet spot — enough CPU and RAM to run a real production workload without overpaying. Pick **Basic** only if you're running a low-traffic site or a personal project, and step up to **Team** once you're hosting several services or seeing consistent resource pressure.

Here's how the two platforms stack up for a developer, split by positioning and by hard facts:

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Stack([cmp1, tbl1])
cmp1 = Comparison("Positioning for developers", [ios1, and1])
ios1 = {name:"iPhone (iOS)", attributes:["One IDE, one language: Xcode + Swift/SwiftUI","Small, predictable test matrix","Strict App Store review — days, occasional rejection","Locked-down OS: no sideloading (with EU exceptions)","Best-in-class Metal, ARKit, accessibility tooling","Users spend more per app","Requires a Mac to build, always"]}
and1 = {name:"Android", attributes:["Android Studio + Kotlin/Compose, builds on any OS","Emulators + real devices, huge device matrix","Play review is faster and softer than Apple's","Sideloading, ADB, root, custom ROMs — fully open","Deeper system APIs, background work, automation","Larger global reach, incl. emerging markets","More fragmentation to test against"]}
tbl1 = Table("Hard facts", ["Axis","iOS","Android"], [["Primary language","Swift (SwiftUI)","Kotlin (Jetpack Compose)"],["IDE","Xcode — macOS only","Android Studio — macOS/Linux/Windows"],["Dev machine","Mac required","Any OS"],["Account fee","99 USD / year","25 USD one-time"],["Store review","Human review, ~1-3 days, strict","Automated + review, hours to ~1 day, lighter"],["Distribution","App Store only","Play, plus APK, F-Droid, enterprise"],["Platform access","Sandboxed, no root","ADB, root, custom ROMs, system APIs"],["Testing burden","Few devices, few OS versions","Thousands of device/OS combos"],["Monetization","Higher ARPU, stronger paid-app culture"],["Reach","Affluent markets","Global volume, incl. price-sensitive markets"]]}
:::

Short version: pick **iOS** if you want a small, stable toolbox and users who pay — and you own a Mac. Pick **Android** if you want open access to the system, cheap entry, broad reach, or you're not on macOS.

For many developers the pragmatic answer is Android-first for reach and platform freedom, then iOS if your revenue model depends on paying users — since both now share the same declarative-UI mental model (SwiftUI vs Compose), the second platform is a smaller jump than it used to be.

