Short version: neither is universally better — PostgreSQL wins on correctness, features and complex queries; MySQL wins on simplicity, mature read-heavy web workloads and ubiquity. Here's the side-by-side:

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Stack([cmp, note])
cmp = Comparison("PostgreSQL vs MySQL", [pg, my])
pg = {name:"PostgreSQL", attributes:["Object-relational, strict ACID, MVCC","Excellent SQL standard compliance","Rich types: JSONB, arrays, ranges, GIS (PostGIS)","Many index types: B-tree, GIN, GiST, BRIN, partial","True serializable isolation; strong constraints","Best for complex queries, analytics, data integrity","PostgreSQL License (MIT-like), fully open source","Steeper tuning/ops learning curve"]}
my = {name:"MySQL", attributes:["Relational, ACID with InnoDB, MVCC","Good SQL compliance, some vendor quirks","JSON supported, fewer native types","Mainly B-tree + full-text indexes","Serializable available but weaker defaults","Best for read-heavy web apps, simple CRUD, LAMP stacks","GPLv2, plus Oracle commercial editions","Very easy to start; huge hosting ecosystem"]}
note = Badge("Default pick when unsure: PostgreSQL", "neutral")
:::

Rule of thumb I'd give: **choose PostgreSQL unless you have a specific reason not to.** Its stricter behavior and richer type/index system pay off as schemas and queries grow, and JSONB means you rarely need a separate document database. Choose **MySQL/MariaDB** when your workload is mostly simple reads and writes, your team already knows it, or you're on a stack (shared hosting, certain managed services, WordPress-style apps) where it's the path of least resistance — it's genuinely faster and simpler for that shape of work.

Two caveats worth knowing: performance differences between them are usually smaller than the differences caused by schema design and indexing, and modern MySQL has closed much of the gap on JSON and window functions — so don't over-index on dated blog benchmarks. If you tell me the workload (OLTP, analytics, JSON-heavy, expected scale), I can give you a firmer recommendation.

